    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.maximum_flow_value - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.maximum_flow_value.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximum_flow_value()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute only the maximum flow value from source to sink.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximum_flow_value()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute only the maximum flow value from source to sink.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.maximum_flow_value()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute only the maximum flow value from source to sink.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute only the maximum flow value from source to sink.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L74424-L74546){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.maximum_flow_value(\n    source,\n    target,\n    capacity=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Maximum flow value](../assets/graphs/maximum-flow-value.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The directed network from the maximum_flow figure, each edge labeled with its capacity. The accent edges A→T and B→T form a minimum cut separating source S from sink T, with capacity 2 + 3. The card shows that cut and the returned maximum flow value 5.0.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThis is a faster variant of `maximum_flow()` when you only need the flow value and not the flow\nassignment on each edge. It runs the same Edmonds-Karp algorithm and reads capacities the\nsame way: with the default `capacity=None`, an edge's `capacity` attribute is used if it\nhas one, otherwise its weight on a weighted graph, and otherwise `1.0`. Parallel edges\npool their capacities and self-loops are ignored.\n\nThe single number answers questions such as \"how much can this network carry from A to\nB?\" and is convenient for comparing alternative designs or scenarios (for example, the\nthroughput before and after an upgrade or a link failure). By the max-flow min-cut\ntheorem it also equals the total capacity of the cheapest set of edges whose removal\nseparates the source from the sink.\n\nOn a directed graph flow only moves along edge direction; on an undirected graph each\nedge can carry flow either way. If the sink cannot be reached, the result is `0.0`.\nThe method does not modify the graph.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node the flow starts from, given as an integer node ID or a string label.\n\n<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node the flow ends at, given as an integer node ID or a string label. Must be a\n    different node than `source=`.\n\n<code>[capacity]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the edge attribute to use as capacity. Every edge must hold a numeric value\n    for it (`\"weight\"` also accepts the edge weight of a weighted graph); otherwise a\n    `ValueError` is raised. If `None` (default), each edge's `capacity` attribute is used\n    when present, then its weight on weighted graphs, and `1.0` otherwise.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The maximum flow value. `0.0` if the sink is unreachable from the source.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If source or sink doesn't exist, they're the same node, any capacity is negative, or\n    an edge lacks the attribute named by `capacity=`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~maximum_flow`) \n\n:   The flow on each edge.\n\n[](`~minimum_cut`) \n\n:   The cut whose value equals the flow.\n\n[](`~minimum_cost_flow`) \n\n:   The cheapest flow of a given amount.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe only path from `S` to `T` passes through `A`, so the flow is limited by the smaller\nof the two edge weights along the way. A direct `S -> T` edge with capacity `3.0` raises\nthe maximum flow from `5.0` to `8.0`:\n\n::: {#e89f88f4 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"S\", \"A\", 10.0), (\"A\", \"T\", 5.0)], directed=True\n)\nbefore = graph.maximum_flow_value(\"S\", \"T\")\ngraph.add_edge(\"S\", \"T\", weight=3.0)\n\nbefore, graph.maximum_flow_value(\"S\", \"T\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(5.0, 8.0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nCapacities can come from any numeric edge attribute via `capacity=`. Here the edges\ncarry a `lanes` attribute, and the narrow middle segment limits the flow to `2.0`:\n\n::: {#67ddb645 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nroads = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"Depot\", \"Bridge\", {\"lanes\": 4}), (\"Bridge\", \"Town\", {\"lanes\": 2})],\n    directed=True,\n)\n\nroads.maximum_flow_value(\"Depot\", \"Town\", capacity=\"lanes\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n2.0\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The pipe the city can least afford to lose\n\n`pipeline()` is a made-up city's water supply, with each pipe's `capacity` in megaliters\na day. At most 45 ML a day reach the city. Closing each pipe in turn shows how much of\nthat would be lost: each of the three pipes along the northern route, from the\nreservoir through North Intake and West Plant, would cost 25, while three pipes could\nclose with no loss at all:\n\n::: {#dd482f73 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline = ec.pipeline()\nfull = pipeline.maximum_flow_value(\"Reservoir\", \"City\")\n\nloss = {}\nfor pipe, source, target in pipeline.get_edge_df().select(\n    \"id\", \"source\", \"target\"\n).rows():\n    closed = ec.pipeline()\n    closed.delete_edge(source, target)\n    loss[pipe] = full - closed.maximum_flow_value(\"Reservoir\", \"City\")\n\nfull, sorted(loss.values(), reverse=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(45.0,\n [25.0,\n  25.0,\n  25.0,\n  20.0,\n  20.0,\n  20.0,\n  15.0,\n  15.0,\n  15.0,\n  5.0,\n  5.0,\n  0.0,\n  0.0,\n  0.0])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#22946c01 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline.set_edge_attrs(\"loss\", loss)\npipeline.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", edge_label_by=\"loss\",\n    edge_width_by=\"loss\", width=700, height=360,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-